{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "09-information-flow.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Information flow",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Label data with security classes, find the class a computed value must get, and check that every flow of information in a program goes up the lattice; then prove the rule for combining inputs.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A payroll program reads salaries, which are secret and about HR, builds a report from them, and writes a summary to a folder the finance team reads. Is that a leak? To answer, every value needs a label saying how sensitive it is, and labels need an order, \"at least as sensitive as\".\n\nA label here has two parts: a level, low or high, and the set of categories the data is about, from $\\{\\mathrm{fin}, \\mathrm{hr}\\}$. One label is below another when it is below in both parts, so the labels are the product of a chain and a powerset:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let Lv = poset({low, high}; low < high)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let Cat = subsets({fin, hr})",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let SC = product(Lv, Cat)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "lattice(SC)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Security classes (Denning)\n> A **security class** is a level and a set of categories, ordered componentwise: level by level, categories by inclusion. Information may flow from class $a$ to class $b$ only when $a \\le b$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Combining inputs",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> A value is computed from a $(\\mathrm{low}, \\{\\mathrm{fin}\\})$ input and a $(\\mathrm{high}, \\{\\})$ input. Which classes could it safely get? Which one should it get?\n\nIt may reveal something about each input, so its class must be at least each of theirs: an upper bound. Every upper bound is safe; the least one keeps the value as widely usable as possible. So a computed value gets the join of its inputs' classes:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "join(SC, (low, {fin}), (high, {}))",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "join(SC, (high, {hr}), (low, {fin}))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Checking flows",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A program's flows form a relation: $x \\to y$ when information about $x$ reaches $y$. It is secure when every flow goes up. The payroll program:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let Fl = rel({salary, report, audit}; salary->report, report->audit)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "secure(SC, Fl; salary->(high, {hr}), report->(high, {hr, fin}), audit->(low, {fin}))",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The audit summary is labelled $(\\mathrm{low}, \\{\\mathrm{fin}\\})$, strictly below the report it reads: the flow goes down, and the graph marks it. Raising the audit's class fixes it:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "secure(SC, Fl; salary->(high, {hr}), report->(high, {hr, fin}), audit->(high, {hr, fin}))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Decisions and data: two lattices compared",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!note] The last lesson and this one\n> Both use a lattice to make \"more restrictive\" precise, but they order different things. In access decisions the lattice orders the *answers* to one request, and combining rules is a join: the policy's answer can only move up as rules are added. Here the lattice orders the *data*, every value carries a class, and the policy is a condition on every flow: a monotone labelling. One decides a request; the other constrains a computation.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In Lean",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "/-- A security class: high or not, and two categories, each present or not. -/\nstructure SecClass where\n  high : Bool\n  fin  : Bool\n  hr   : Bool\n  deriving Repr, DecidableEq\n\n/-- a may flow to b: componentwise, false below true. -/\ndef SecClass.le (a b : SecClass) : Bool := (!a.high || b.high) && (!a.fin || b.fin) && (!a.hr || b.hr)\ndef SecClass.join (a b : SecClass) : SecClass := ⟨a.high || b.high, a.fin || b.fin, a.hr || b.hr⟩\n\n/-- The join is the least upper bound: data may flow from the join exactly when it may flow from both. -/\ntheorem join_le_iff (a b c : SecClass) : (a.join b).le c = (a.le c && b.le c) := by\n  cases a; cases b; cases c\n  rename_i a1 a2 a3 b1 b2 b3 c1 c2 c3\n  cases a1 <;> cases a2 <;> cases a3 <;> cases b1 <;> cases b2 <;> cases b3 <;> cases c1 <;> cases c2 <;> cases c3 <;> rfl",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem join_flow_left (a b c : SecClass) (h : (a.join b).le c = true) : a.le c = true := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "If a computation's output may flow to $c$, so may its first input.",
      "leanSolution": "  rw [join_le_iff] at h\n  exact (Bool.and_eq_true _ _ |>.mp h).1",
      "hints": [
        "Rewrite `h` with `join_le_iff`.",
        "`Bool.and_eq_true` splits a conjunction of Booleans."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "join(SC, (low, {fin}), (high, {hr}))",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "What class does a value computed from $(\\mathrm{low}, \\{\\mathrm{fin}\\})$ and $(\\mathrm{high}, \\{\\mathrm{hr}\\})$ data get? Write it as `(level, {categories})`.",
      "hints": [
        "Join componentwise: the higher level, the union of the categories."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "le(SC, (high, {}), (low, {fin}))",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "May information flow from $(\\mathrm{high}, \\{\\})$ to $(\\mathrm{low}, \\{\\mathrm{fin}\\})$? Answer `true` or `false`.",
      "hints": [
        "Compare the levels."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> Security classes form a product lattice; data flows only upward, a computation's output gets the join of its inputs' classes, and a program is secure when its labelling is monotone along every flow.\n\nSecurity classes are a lattice we designed. Often there are two orders already, a fine one and a coarse one, and the question is how to translate between them as faithfully as possible. Next: Galois connections.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
